    {
      icon:"atom", hl:true, title:"Cell Differentiation and the Evolution of Multicellularity",
      blocks:[
        {type:"prose", text:"Every cell in a multicellular organism carries the same genome, yet becomes a highly specialized cell type by switching different genes on or off &mdash; a division of labour that buys efficiency at the cost of making each specialized cell completely dependent on the others."},
        {type:"callout", style:"definition", icon:"book", title:"Definition", text:"When a cell becomes specialized it is said to be <strong>differentiating</strong>: certain genes are activated and expressed, depending on the cell&rsquo;s developmental history, its position in the developing organism, and signals from its immediate environment, while other genes remain switched off. The nucleus of every cell holds all the information needed to build any cell type in the whole organism, but only a selected part of it is ever used in any one cell or tissue."},
        {type:"prose", text:"Specialized cells are organized into <strong>tissues</strong> (collections of cells of similar structure and function, e.g. heart muscle or phloem) and <strong>organs</strong> (collections of different tissues performing a coordinated function, e.g. the heart or a leaf)."},
        {type:"prose", text:"Because the genome is identical in every cell of an organism &mdash; all descended by division from one original cell &mdash; but each cell type expresses a different selection of its genes, each cell type also produces a different <strong>proteome</strong>: the entire set of proteins a genome expresses in a cell, tissue or organism at a given time, which varies with a cell&rsquo;s function, location and environment."},
        {type:"callout", style:"example", icon:"leaf", title:"Example", text:"Specialization achieves efficiency through <strong>division of labour</strong>, but at a cost: a specialized cell becomes wholly dependent on other specialized cells for functions it can no longer perform itself &mdash; a nerve cell, for instance, is adapted to transmit nerve impulses, but depends entirely on red blood cells for oxygen and on heart muscle cells to pump its blood supply. This is one more reason why no single, &lsquo;typical&rsquo; cell really exists."},
        {type:"prose", text:"Multicellularity itself has evolved independently many times &mdash; in animals, plants, most fungi, and some algae &mdash; suggesting it is relatively easy to evolve, probably because only a few genetic changes, enabling cell adhesion, cell-to-cell communication and differentiation, are needed to unlock its two big advantages: a larger possible body size, and the efficiency gains of cell specialization."}
      ],
      notes:[{label:"Common mistake", text:"A full answer on differentiation must mention that specific genes are being selectively expressed and others suppressed &mdash; simply stating that &lsquo;cells specialize&rsquo; is too vague to earn credit; name a specific tissue and its function wherever possible."}]
    }
