  summary: [
    "The cell is the smallest unit of life, and cell theory holds that all organisms are built from one or more cells, which can only arise from pre-existing cells &mdash; though a few genuine exceptions exist.",
    "Cell dimensions are measured in micrometres (&micro;m) and nanometres (nm); a plant cell averages about 40 &micro;m and an animal cell about 20 &micro;m, while organelles and molecules range from a 10&ndash;20 &micro;m nucleus down to a 2 nm DNA molecule.",
    "Magnification (image size &divide; actual size) tells you how many times larger an image is; resolution &mdash; the ability to see two close points as separate &mdash; is what actually limits the detail visible, and depends on wavelength, not magnification.",
    "The light microscope resolves to about 0.2 &micro;m; the electron microscope, using a much shorter-wavelength electron beam through a vacuum on dead material, resolves to about 5 nm, revealing the detailed ultrastructure of organelles invisible by light microscopy.",
    "Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) passes a beam through a thin stained section; scanning electron microscopy (SEM) scans a beam across a surface to build a 3-D image; cryogenic electron microscopy flash-freezes biomolecules for near-atomic detail; freeze fracture compares a fractured, replica-coated surface against thin-sectioned material to confirm structures are real, not artefacts; and fluorescence microscopy (including immunofluorescence) uses filtered light and labelled dyes or antibodies to pinpoint specific molecules.",
    "Prokaryotic cells (bacteria) have no true nucleus &mdash; their DNA forms a single loop in a nucleoid &mdash; and lack membrane-bound organelles; every bacterium has a peptidoglycan cell wall, a plasma membrane, cytoplasm, naked circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, with structures like capsules, plasmids, flagella and pili present only in some species.",
    "Eukaryotic cells (animals, plants, fungi, protists) have a true, membrane-bound nucleus and numerous membrane-bound organelles &mdash; the prokaryote/eukaryote split is the single most fundamental division in cell biology.",
    "The nucleus (10&ndash;20 &micro;m) holds chromosomes &mdash; DNA bound to histones &mdash; in a diffuse chromatin state for most of a cell&rsquo;s life, and contains nucleoli, which assemble ribosome subunits; mitochondria (double membrane, cristae, matrix) are the site of aerobic respiration; ribosomes (70S or 80S) are the universal site of protein synthesis.",
    "The endoplasmic reticulum (rough ER makes proteins for export; smooth ER makes lipids), the Golgi apparatus (processes and packages biochemicals into vesicles) and lysosomes (digest food, waste and defunct organelles, and drive autolysis and apoptosis) together form a single production and recycling pathway.",
    "The plasma membrane (a 7 nm lipid bilayer) is the universal boundary of every cell; the cytoskeleton&rsquo;s microtubules (spindle fibres, organelle transport) and microfilaments (cleavage furrow, muscle contraction, cytoplasmic streaming) give the cell shape and move its contents.",
    "Unicellular organisms such as <em>Paramecium</em> and <em>Chlorella</em> carry out all eight functions of life &mdash; homeostasis, metabolism, nutrition, movement, excretion, growth, sensitivity and reproduction &mdash; within a single cell, using structures such as the contractile vacuole for osmotic homeostasis.",
    "Animal cells have centrioles, centrosomes and, in some cells, cilia or flagella; plant cells have chloroplasts, plastids, a permanent vacuole with a tonoplast, and a cellulose cell wall; fungal cells have a chitin cell wall. Atypical exceptions to cell theory include multinucleate hyphae (<em>Mucor</em>) and muscle fibres (syncytia), and the nucleus-free red blood cell and phloem sieve tube element.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> the endosymbiotic theory (Lynn Margulis, 1967) proposes that mitochondria and chloroplasts are descendants of free-living prokaryotes engulfed by, and retained within, an ancestral host cell &mdash; supported by their binary fission, naked circular DNA, 70S ribosomes, own protein synthesis, size and double membrane, with DNA-sequence evidence specifically linking mitochondria to the alphaproteobacteria.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> cell differentiation activates a different selection of genes in each cell type, producing a distinct proteome for each despite an identical genome, and underlies the division of labour between specialized cells and tissues &mdash; at the cost of making each specialized cell dependent on others.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> multicellularity has evolved independently many times across the eukaryotic tree of life, likely because relatively few genetic changes &mdash; enabling adhesion, communication and differentiation &mdash; are needed to unlock its advantages of larger body size and cell specialization."
  ]
